<!--
  Skeletal (fill-in) version of the "Why the Second Derivative Test
  Works" subsection, included by 4.7.

  This subsection is one continuous derivation, so almost all of it
  becomes workspace: the headings and the statement of what each step is
  for are kept, and the algebra is left to the reader.  Content mirrors
  why-second-derivative-test.ptx of the full lecture notes; every xml:id
  here is prefixed with "skel-" so the two can coexist.

  Figure: the animation of the rotating slice is kept, since it is how a
  reader checks the sign argument they have just written out.
-->
<subsection xml:id="subsec-skel-fmv-why-second-derivative-test">
<title>Why the Second Derivative Test Works</title>

<p>
Where do the conditions of
<xref ref="thm-skel-second-derivative-test"/> come from? The key idea is
to take the directional derivative <em>twice</em>. At a critical point
every direction looks flat to the first derivative, so we ask the second
derivative how the surface <em>curves</em> as we walk away from the point
in each direction. The video animates the argument; the equations that
appear in it are the ones you will derive below.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-skel-why-sdt-video">
<caption>
Slicing a surface through a critical point in a rotating unit
direction <m>\mathbf{u}</m>. The second directional derivative
<m>D^2_{\mathbf{u}}f</m> is the curvature of the slice: one sign for
all directions gives a local extremum, both signs give a saddle point,
and completing the square shows that the discriminant
<m>H = f_{xx}f_{yy} - f_{xy}^2</m> decides between the two.
</caption>
<video xml:id="video-skel-why-sdt"
       source="videos/second_derivative_test_1080p.mp4"
       width="70%" aspect="16:9"/>
</figure>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<paragraphs xml:id="skel-par-sdt-slice">
<title>Slicing the surface in a direction <m>\mathbf{u}</m></title>

<p>
Suppose <m>f(x,y)</m> has continuous first and second partial
derivatives near a critical point <m>(a,b)</m>, so that
<m>f_x(a,b) = f_y(a,b) = 0</m>. Fix a unit vector
<m>\mathbf{u} = (u_1, u_2)</m> and walk away from the critical point
along the line through <m>(a,b)</m> in the direction of
<m>\mathbf{u}</m>. Write down the height of the surface above that line
as a single-variable function <m>g(t)</m>. (Parametrize the line as in
<xref ref="eq-skel-line-through-P0"/>, then feed it into <m>f</m>. This
<m>g</m> is the <term>slice</term> cut by the vertical plane through
<m>(a,b)</m> containing <m>\mathbf u</m>.)
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,5);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,4.7) {$g(t) =$};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank workspace for the single-variable function that gives the height of the surface along the line.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>
</paragraphs>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<paragraphs xml:id="skel-par-sdt-first">
<title>The first directional derivative</title>

<p>
Differentiate <m>g</m> once, and evaluate at the critical point. (Use
<xref ref="eq-skel-chain-rule-two"/>; the two inner derivatives are
constants, read straight off the parametrization. Recognize the result as
something already named in
<xref ref="subsec-skel-gradient-vector"/>. Then set <m>t = 0</m> and use
the hypothesis <mdash/> the answer is the same for <em>every</em>
<m>\mathbf u</m>, which is exactly why one derivative cannot tell the
three cases apart. Say that in a sentence.)
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,10);
\draw[gray!35] (0,6.5) -- (15,6.5);
\draw[gray!35] (0,3.5) -- (15,3.5);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,9.7) {$g'(t) =$};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,6.2) {$g'(0) =$ \qquad for every $\mathbf u$, because:};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,3.2) {So the first derivative cannot distinguish:};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank box in three rows, for the first derivative of the slice, for its value at the critical point, and for why that value settles nothing.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>
</paragraphs>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<paragraphs xml:id="skel-par-sdt-second">
<title>The second directional derivative</title>

<p>
Differentiate once more. (Apply the chain rule to each of the two terms
of <m>g'(t)</m>, remembering that <m>f_x</m> and <m>f_y</m> are
themselves functions of <m>x</m> and <m>y</m>. Four second partials
appear, but <xref ref="thm-skel-mixed-derivative"/> lets two of them be
combined; the result is a quadratic in <m>u_1</m> and <m>u_2</m>.)
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,11);
\draw[gray!35] (0,4) -- (15,4);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,10.7) {Differentiating again:};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,3.7) {$D^2_{\mathbf u} f =$};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank box in two rows, for differentiating the first directional derivative and for the resulting quadratic expression.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>

<assemblage xml:id="skel-summary-sdt-second">
<title>The Second Directional Derivative</title>
<p>
<md xml:id="eq-skel-sdt-second" number="yes">
D^2_{\mathbf{u}}f
= f_{xx}\, u_1^2 + 2 f_{xy}\, u_1 u_2 + f_{yy}\, u_2^2 .
</md>
</p>
</assemblage>

<p>
The number <m>g''(0) = D^2_{\mathbf{u}}f(a,b)</m> is the
<term>curvature of the slice</term> at the critical point. Say what its
sign means, and then what the single-variable second derivative test,
applied to <m>g</m>, gives in each of three cases. (A positive value
means the slice is concave one way, a negative value the other. Then ask
what must be true <em>across all directions</em> for a local minimum, for
a local maximum, and for a saddle.)
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,11);
\draw[gray!35] (0,8) -- (15,8);
\draw[gray!35] (0,5.5) -- (15,5.5);
\draw[gray!35] (0,3) -- (15,3);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,10.7) {Positive means \dots \qquad negative means \dots};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,7.7) {Local minimum if:};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,5.2) {Local maximum if:};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,2.7) {Saddle point if:};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank box in four rows, for the meaning of the sign of the second directional derivative and for the condition across all directions in each of the three cases.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>

<p>
Writing <m>\mathbf{u} = (\cos\theta, \sin\theta)</m> turns
<xref ref="eq-skel-sdt-second"/> into a function of the direction angle
alone. Write it down, and evaluate it for the three surfaces of
<xref ref="ex-skel-marble-stability"/>. (Two of the three come out
<em>constant</em> in <m>\theta</m>, which is the whole story for those
surfaces; the third does not, and a double-angle identity puts it in a
form where you can count how often it changes sign in a full turn.)
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,11);
\draw[gray!35] (0,7.5) -- (15,7.5);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,10.7) {$D^2_{\mathbf u} f(\theta) =$};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,7.2) {For (i): \qquad for (ii): \qquad for the saddle:};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank box in two rows, for the second directional derivative as a function of the direction angle and for its value on each of the three sample surfaces.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>
</paragraphs>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<paragraphs xml:id="skel-par-sdt-square">
<title>Completing the square: the discriminant appears</title>

<p>
Checking the sign of <xref ref="eq-skel-sdt-second"/> one direction at a
time is impossible; there are infinitely many directions. Instead,
assume <m>f_{xx} \neq 0</m>, multiply <xref ref="eq-skel-sdt-second"/> by
<m>f_{xx}</m>, and complete the square in <m>u_1</m>. (Multiplying first
is what makes the square come out with integer coefficients. Group the
<m>u_1^2</m> and <m>u_1u_2</m> terms into a perfect square, and see what
has to be added back. Check your work by expanding the square again.)
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,12);
\draw[gray!35] (0,8) -- (15,8);
\draw[gray!35] (0,3.5) -- (15,3.5);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,11.7) {$f_{xx}\cdot D^2_{\mathbf u} f =$};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,7.7) {Completing the square:};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,3.2) {Check by expanding:};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank box in three rows, for the product with the second partial derivative, for completing the square, and for the check.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>

<assemblage xml:id="skel-summary-sdt-square">
<title>The Completed Square</title>
<p>
<md xml:id="eq-skel-sdt-square" number="yes">
f_{xx}\cdot D^2_{\mathbf{u}}f
= \left(f_{xx} u_1 + f_{xy} u_2\right)^2
+ \left(f_{xx} f_{yy} - f_{xy}^2\right) u_2^2 .
</md>
</p>
</assemblage>

<p>
Now say what has been gained. (Every term on the right of
<xref ref="eq-skel-sdt-square"/> is a square except one coefficient
<mdash/> name it, and match it against
<xref ref="eq-skel-hessian"/>. How many numbers now control the sign of
<m>D^2_{\mathbf u}f</m> in <em>all</em> directions at once, and which
are they?)
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,6);
\draw[gray!35] (0,3) -- (15,3);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,5.7) {The one coefficient that is not a square:};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,2.7) {The sign in all directions is controlled by:};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank box in two rows, for the coefficient that is not a perfect square and for the two numbers that control the sign in every direction.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>
</paragraphs>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<paragraphs xml:id="skel-par-sdt-cases">
<title>Reading off the four cases</title>

<p>
Work through the three cases from
<xref ref="eq-skel-sdt-square"/>.
</p>

<p>
(For <m>H \gt 0</m>: both terms on the right are non-negative, and they
cannot vanish together <mdash/> check the two possibilities
<m>u_2 = 0</m> and <m>u_2 \neq 0</m> separately. Conclude that
<m>D^2_{\mathbf u}f</m> has a fixed sign, and say which. Note also that
<m>H \gt 0</m> rules out <m>f_{xx} = 0</m>; show why.)
</p>

<p>
(For <m>H \lt 0</m>: the two terms now compete, so find <em>two</em>
directions giving opposite signs. One is <m>\mathbf u = (1,0)</m>; for
the other, choose <m>\mathbf u</m> so that the perfect square vanishes
<mdash/> the bracket in <xref ref="eq-skel-sdt-square"/> tells you which
direction that is.)
</p>

<p>
(For <m>H = 0</m>: what does <xref ref="eq-skel-sdt-square"/> collapse
to, and in which direction does it vanish? Say what the second derivative
can and cannot tell you there.)
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,18);
\draw[gray!35] (0,12) -- (15,12);
\draw[gray!35] (0,6) -- (15,6);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,17.7) {$H &gt; 0$:};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,11.7) {$H &lt; 0$:};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,5.7) {$H = 0$:};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank box in three labelled rows, one for each case of the sign of the discriminant.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>

<p>
These are precisely conditions (i)<ndash/>(iv) of
<xref ref="thm-skel-second-derivative-test"/>. One technical remark: the
argument above examines only straight-line slices through
<m>(a,b)</m>, which by itself is not quite enough (a function can
increase along every line yet fail to have a local minimum). The full
proof replaces <m>f</m> near <m>(a,b)</m> by its second-order Taylor
expansion; continuity of the second partials guarantees that when
<m>H \neq 0</m> the quadratic form <m>D^2_{\mathbf{u}}f</m> keeps its
sign uniformly on a small disk around <m>(a,b)</m>, which upgrades the
line-by-line conclusion to a genuine local one.
</p>
</paragraphs>

</subsection>
